\begin_layout Section*
4.5
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Cost minimization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the context of the Production function model, we can unambiguously refer
 to inputs and hence keep track of a firm's costs.
 In this section we characterize a firm's profit maximizaiton problem by
 means of its cost minimization problem.
 For simplicity, we will concentrate in the single-ouput case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider a firm represented by a production function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

.
 Imagine that the firm decides to produce 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 units of output and input prices are 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 The firm's Cost Minimization Problem for 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \min_{\{z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:f(z)\geq q\}}}\ w\cdot z
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
Cost function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(100).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 be a production function.
 The 
\series bold
cost function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
c^{f}: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & (w,q) & \mapsto & c^{f}(w,q)\equiv{\displaystyle \min_{\{z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:f(z)\geq q\}}}\ w\cdot z
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Conditional factor demand correspondence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(101).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 be a production function.
 The 
\series bold
conditional factor demand correspondence associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
z^{f}: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}\\
 & (w,q) & \mapsto & z^{f}(w,q)\equiv{\displaystyle \arg\min_{\{z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:f(z)\geq q\}}}\ w\cdot z
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(53).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 be a production function, 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|z^{f}(w,q)\right|=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{f}(w,q)\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 Then:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
For each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{l,k\}\subseteq\{1,\ldots,L-1\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{w_{l}}{w_{k}}=MRTS_{lk}(z^{f}(w,q))
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is the Lagrange Multiplier associated with the Cost Minimization Problem
 for the constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $q-f(z)\leq0$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial c}{\partial q}(w,q)=\left|\lambda\right|
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
 Check Mas-Colell, Chapter 5 (Cost Minimization).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The following are properties of a cost function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(54).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 be a production function.
 Then:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $c^{f}$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree one in 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{f}(\cdot,q)$
\end_inset

 is a concave function, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{f}$
\end_inset

 is a concave function of 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If for each 
\begin_inset Formula $q>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}:f(z)\geq q\}$
\end_inset

 are convex sets, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y^{f}\equiv\left\{ (-z,q):z\geq0,\ \forall\ w\gg0,\ w\cdot z\geq c(w,q)\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $z^{f}$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree zero in 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If for each 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}:f(z)\geq q\}$
\end_inset

 is convex, then 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{f}$
\end_inset

 is convex-valued.
 If for each 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}:f(z)\geq q\}$
\end_inset

 is strictly convex, then 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{f}$
\end_inset

 is single-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
(Sheppard's Lemma) Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|z^{f}(w,q)\right|=1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{f}$
\end_inset

 is differentiable with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $(w,q)$
\end_inset

 and for each 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{1,\ldots,L-1\}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial c^{f}}{\partial w_{l}}(w,q)=z_{l}^{f}(w,q)
\]

\end_inset

or in matrix notation,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\nabla_{w}c(w,q)=z(w,q)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{f}$
\end_inset

 is a differentiable function in a neighborhood of 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

, then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $D_{w}z^{f}(w,q)w=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $D_{w}z^{f}(w,q)=D_{w}^{2}c(w,q)$
\end_inset

 is symmetric and negative semi-definite.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree one, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $Y^{f}$
\end_inset

 satisfies CR, then 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{f}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{f}$
\end_inset

 are homogenous of degree one in 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is concave, then for each 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{f}(w,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a convex function, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{f}$
\end_inset

 is a convex function of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
 Check Mas-Colell Proposition 5.C.2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
4.5.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Single-input and single-output model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section, we study the single-input and single-output model.
 The simplicity of this environment allows us to graphically characterize
 a firm's cost minimization and profit maximization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider a firm represented by the production function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}_{+}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 be the output price and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 be the input price.
 In order to simplify notation, we consider 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 to be fixed and denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{f}(w,q)$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $c(q)$
\end_inset

.
 Likewise, we denote 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{f}(w,p)$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(p)$
\end_inset

.
 The PMP is then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{q\geq0}}\ pq-c(q)
\]

\end_inset

Assume that this problem has a unique solution 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(p)$
\end_inset

.
 If this solution is interior and the cost function is differentiable, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p=c^{\prime}(Q(p))
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(102).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Then
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $AC(q)\equiv\frac{c(q)}{q}$
\end_inset

 is the average cost at 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $MC(c)\equiv c^{\prime}(q)$
\end_inset

 is the marginal cost at 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
4.5.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Long-run vs.
 short-run
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that a firm is represented by a production set 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

.
 If there is free entry of firms to this market, this firm can be replciated
 as many times as possible.
 Thus, in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

long-run,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the aggregate technical restrictions are given by the set
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
AY\equiv\{y_{1}+\cdots+y_{n}:n\in\mathbb{N},\ \forall\ i=1,\ldots,n,\ y_{i}\in Y\}
\]

\end_inset

This is the aggregate produciton set, which describes the feasible production
 plans for an industry, as a whole.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(55).

\series default
 
\emph on
If 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies NI returns to scale, then 
\begin_inset Formula $AY$
\end_inset

 satisfies CR to scale.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
 Check Problem 2, Section 4.4 for a basic outline of this proof.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under additional technical conditions, one can guarantee that if 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is represented by a production functions that is homogeneous of degree
 one, then the supply correspondence is flat.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Multiple input case with temporarily fixed inputs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In situations in which there are multiple inputs, some of these may be temporari
ly fixed.
 Economists refer to these situations as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

short-run.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Consistently, they refer to situations in which no input is fixed in the
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

long-run.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For instance, suppose that there are two inputs: labor, denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

, and capital, denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

long-run
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 production function is simply the production function as we have defined
 it in our production model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(L,K)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}\mapsto f(L,K)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}
\]

\end_inset

For a fixed amount of capital, say 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{K}$
\end_inset

, the short-run production function is 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\bar{K}}$
\end_inset

 defined by, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\bar{K}}(L)\equiv f(L,\bar{K})$
\end_inset

.
 The cost function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\bar{K}}$
\end_inset

 is the short-run cost function at 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{K}$
\end_inset

.
 One can easily graphically characterize the long-run cost and average cost
 functions, as the lower envelope of short-run cost and short-run average
 cost functions.
 Clearly, under this interpretation, long-run supply correspondences are
 not necessarily flat.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
4.6
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Aggregate production
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{J}\}$
\end_inset

 be a family of production sts.
 The aggregate production set associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\{Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{J}\}$
\end_inset

 is the set
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{AG}\equiv Y_{1}+\cdots+Y_{J}\equiv\left\{ y_{1}+\cdots+y_{J}:y_{1}\in y_{1},\ldots,y_{J}\in Y_{J}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

The aggregate supply correspondence associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\{Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{J}\}$
\end_inset

 is the correspondence that associated with each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

, the set
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{AG}(p)\equiv y^{Y_{1}}(p)+\cdots+y^{Y_{J}}(p)\equiv\left\{ y_{1}+\cdots+y_{J}:y_{1}\in y^{Y_{1}},\ldots,y_{J}\in y^{Y_{J}}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(56).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{J}\right\} $
\end_inset

 be a family of production sets.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $y^{Y_{AG}}(p)=y_{AG}(p)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi^{Y_{AG}}(p)=\Pi^{Y_{1}}(p)+\cdots+\Pi^{Y_{J}}(p)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in y_{AG}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $y=y_{1}+\cdots+y_{J}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}\in y^{Y_{1}},\ldots,y_{J}\in y^{Y_{J}}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y_{AG}$
\end_inset

 and consequently, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y\leq\Pi^{Y_{AG}}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot y=\Pi^{Y_{1}}(p)+\cdots+\Pi^{Y_{J}}(p)\leq\Pi^{Y_{AG}}(p)
\]

\end_inset

We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y\geq\Pi^{Y_{AG}}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose by contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y<\Pi^{Y_{AG}}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, there is 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\equiv y_{1}^{\prime}+\cdots+y_{J}^{\prime}\in Y_{AG}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y<p\cdot y^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, there is 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\{1,\ldots,J\}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{j}^{\prime}>p\cdot y_{j}$
\end_inset

 for otherwise 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{1}^{\prime}+\cdots+p\cdots y_{J}^{\prime}\leq p\cdot y_{1}+\cdots+p\cdot y_{J}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}\notin y^{Y_{j}}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 This is a contradiction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in y^{Y_{AG}}(p)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y=\Pi^{Y_{AG}}(p)=\Pi^{Y_{1}}(p)+\cdots+\Pi^{Y_{J}}(p)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, let 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in y^{Y_{AG}}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y=\Pi^{Y_{AG}}$
\end_inset

 and there are 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}\in Y_{1},\ldots,y_{J}\in Y_{J}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $y=y_{1}+\cdots+y_{J}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\{1,\ldots,J\}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{j}\leq\Pi^{Y_{j}}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}\in y^{Y_{j}}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction that for some 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{*}\in\{1,\ldots,J\}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{j^{*}}<\Pi^{Y_{j^{*}}}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{1}+\cdots+p\cdot y_{J}<\Pi^{Y_{1}}(p)+\cdots+\Pi^{Y_{J}}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi^{Y_{AG}}<\Pi^{Y_{1}}(p)+\cdots+\Pi^{Y_{J}}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 This is a contradiction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
4.7
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Efficient production
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(103).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 be a production set.
 A feasible vector 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y$
\end_inset

 is efficient if there is no 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\in Y$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\gneq y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(57).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y$
\end_inset

 is profit maximizing at some 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is convex.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y$
\end_inset

 is efficient for 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

, then it is profit maxizing for some 
\begin_inset Formula $p\geq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 be a production set and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y$
\end_inset

 is profit maximizing at some 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is efficient.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is not efficient.
 Then, there is 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\in Y$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\neq y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\geq y$
\end_inset

, Because 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

, this implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y^{\prime'}>p\cdot y$
\end_inset

, contradicting the assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is profit maximizing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 be a non-empty convex production set that satisfies free disposal.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 be efficient at 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

, i.e., there is no 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\in Y$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\gneq y$
\end_inset

 We prove that there is 
\begin_inset Formula $p\geq0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is profit maximizing at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we show that 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is a boundary point of 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\overline{Y}\cap\overline{Y^{c}}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $y\notin\overline{Y}\cap\overline{Y^{c}}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mbox{Int}(Y)$
\end_inset

, which by definition is an open set.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\epsilon}(y)\subseteq\mbox{Int}(Y)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}:\tau_{l}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}=y+\left(\nicefrac{\epsilon}{2}\right)\tau$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\in B_{\epsilon}(y)$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 However, 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\geq y$
\end_inset

, which contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is efficient in 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\overline{Y}\cap\overline{Y^{c}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, by the Basic Supporting Hyperplane Theorem, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ p\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y^{\prime}\in Y$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y^{\prime}\leq p\cdot y$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}=y-\epsilon v_{l}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 that has 1 in the 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

-th component and zeros for 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq l$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y\geq\tilde{y}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies free disposal, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 As a result, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y\geq p\cdot\tilde{y}$
\end_inset

, which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot(y-\tilde{y})\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot v_{l}\geq0$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}\geq0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Therefore, there is 
\begin_inset Formula $p\geq0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is profit maximizing at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

